<h1>Orcraft Result Fields</h1>
<p>A result reports whatever measurements its component chose to declare, on top of the status and
  descriptions every result carries. This page covers the declared fields, the inherited ones, and the
  counters each level sums from the level below. How the status itself is set is covered in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/">Orcraft Statuses</a>.</p>

<h2 id="result-fields">Result fields</h2>
<p>Result fields come from the <code>OrcraftResultField</code> namespace and are declared on a result
  class, exactly the way config fields are declared on a config class:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")
	within_limits = OrcraftResultField.Boolean(default=False, label="Within Limits")
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")
	log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(
		default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL
	)') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Text</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Numeric</code></td>
      <td><code>int</code> or <code>float</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Boolean</code></td>
      <td><code>bool</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Format</code></td>
      <td>Presentation-only, used for <code>log_orientation</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Each accepts <code>default</code>, <code>default_factory</code>, <code>label</code> and
  <code>formatter</code>, with the same meanings they have for
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#options">config fields</a>. A field with
  a <code>label</code> appears in the logged result table and in the controller; one without stays
  internal.</p>

<p>A <code>formatter</code> changes how a value is displayed without changing what is stored, which is
  how a raw number becomes a percentage or a status becomes a colored word in the log.</p>

<h2 id="plain-attributes">Plain attributes</h2>
<p>The same holds on the way out. A result can carry plain attributes alongside its declared fields, and
  the framework hands them to the caller without ever showing them:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyProduceStepResult(OrcraftTestStepResult):
	item_count = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Item Count")

	# Plain attributes: for the calling flow, never a result column.
	calibration = None
	channel_map = ()


class MyProduceStep(OrcraftTestStep):
	def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
		self.result = MyProduceStepResult()
		self.result.item_count = len(items)
		self.result.calibration = {"gain": 0.98, "offset": -0.02}
		self.result.channel_map = (0, 2, 1, 3)
		return self.result') }}

<p>Declared fields are the step's visible surface: they appear in the logged result table and as result
  columns in the controller. Plain attributes are private to the flow that ran the step. They come back
  with the result - including from a remote endpoint, with their Python types intact - but they are
  deliberately excluded from the result columns, because they are often exactly the kind of thing nobody
  wants as a column: a whole state grid, a buffer, megabytes of structure that the next step needs and
  a table would render as noise.</p>

<p>Anything on the result that is not an Orcraft field, not underscored and not a method counts as a
  plain attribute - whether it was declared on the class or assigned in the body.</p>

<h2 id="inherited-fields">Inherited fields</h2>
<p>Result classes inherit a set of fields they never declare. <em>Overridden by user</em> marks the ones
  your code may assign - none of them has to be, since every result is complete without them.
  <em>Reserved</em> marks the ones the framework writes as the run progresses, and its icon says how
  strictly: <span class="mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</span> is a field the framework sets on
  your behalf - through a checkpoint, an exception - that you may still set directly;
  <span class="mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</span> is fully reserved, and redeclaring
  it changes what the log and the controller report. See the per-component pages for the counters each
  level adds on top.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Overridden by user (optional)</th>
      <th class="mark-head">Reserved</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>status</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</td>
      <td>The component's outcome - one of the values listed under <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#statuses">Statuses</a>. The framework derives it from checkpoints and descriptions; assign it yourself only for <code class="status-error">ERROR</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-fail">failure_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</td>
      <td>Why the system under test was judged incorrect. Set by you or by a failing checkpoint; setting it moves a passing result to <code class="status-fail">FAIL</code> on its own.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-error">error_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</td>
      <td>Why the component could not complete. Set by you, or by the framework on an uncaught exception.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code class="status-warning">warning_description</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td>Something worth flagging that did not change the status.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>finish_time</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>When the component finished.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>elapsed_time</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>How long it took.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td>Whether the result table is logged horizontally, vertically or not at all (<code>OFF</code>). Override its default in code.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--none">&ndash;</td>
      <td class="docs-table__number mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Fields you may set are assigned at run time - <code>case.result.warning_description = "..."</code> -
    not redeclared. Redeclare only to change a default, and then with the same
    <code>OrcraftResultField</code> type:
    <code>log_orientation = OrcraftResultField.Format(default=OrcraftResultField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL)</code>.
    A bare value on the class shadows the field, and the log table and the controller no longer see it.</p>
</div>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under one of the reserved names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps
    writing to the name, but through your definition - and status comparisons, the counters rolled up
    from below, and the controller's result columns silently break.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="aggregation">Aggregated counters</h2>
<p>Each level above a case counts what happened beneath it, so a summary is available without any code
  keeping score:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Result</th>
      <th>Counters</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestResult</code></td>
      <td><code>total_cases</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_cases</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_cases</code>, <code class="status-error">error_cases</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_cases</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code></td>
      <td>The same case counters, plus <code>total_tests</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_tests</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_tests</code>, <code class="status-error">error_tests</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_tests</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OrcraftTestSuiteResult</code></td>
      <td>The case and test counters, plus <code>total_groups</code>, <code class="status-pass">passed_groups</code>, <code class="status-fail">failed_groups</code>, <code class="status-error">error_groups</code>, <code class="status-warning">warning_groups</code></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Because case counts carry all the way to the top, a suite result answers both questions at once: how
  many of its groups came back clean, and how many individual cases were actually executed to get
  there.</p>
